    ('bdc47035-70e0-4126-b9f0-698d294c7cc9'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Horncastle was fortified, yet lay on no important Roman road, which suggests the River Bain was the main way in. Today it lies 21 miles from Lincoln and 13 miles from Louth.', 'Although fortified, Horncastle was not on any important Roman roads, which suggests that the River Bain was the principal route of access to it.', 'teaser-card-s005-rollback:bdc47035-70e0-4126-b9f0-698d294c7cc9:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s005: card_description ''Although fortified, Horncastle was not on any important Roman roads, which suggests that the River Bain was the principal route of access to it.'' restored on Horncastle', 'fc36298d4e36e543194ad81687ac72e5ffcd1ee869f1fa7059396f85b8c2998e|b3860837e288cf9578d4dff04d7a0164a58368329ad143247e606c70dd1b821e|b3860837e288cf9578d4dff04d7a0164a58368329ad143247e606c70dd1b821e', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-023", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Horncastle was fortified, yet lay on no important Roman road, which suggests the River Bain was the main way in. Today it lies 21 miles from Lincoln and 13 miles from Louth."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-023 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Horncastle was fortified [S1]; it lay on no important Roman road [S1]; this suggests the River Bain was the main route of access [S1]; today it lies 21 miles from Lincoln [S2]; today it lies 13 miles from Louth [S2]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-068 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Horncastle (in England) was a fortified Roman place, with Roman walls [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Horncastle]; Horncastle lay on no important Roman road [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Horncastle]; That suggests the River Bain was the main way in [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Horncastle]; Today Horncastle lies 21 miles from Lincoln [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Horncastle]; Today Horncastle lies 13 miles from Louth [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Horncastle]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('be29b127-7a5f-48fe-b1f9-ad4f67fe9c55'::uuid, 'card_description', 'On a hill over Dumat al-Jandal rises Marid Castle, stone below and mudbrick above. Queen Zenobia of Palmyra is reported to have besieged the city without being able to capture the fortress.', 'A fortress so formidable that Queen Zenobia of Palmyra laid siege to it and failed to break through. She declared ''he who reaches Marid has indeed attained power.''', 'teaser-card-s005-rollback:be29b127-7a5f-48fe-b1f9-ad4f67fe9c55:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s005: card_description "A fortress so formidable that Queen Zenobia of Palmyra laid siege to it and failed to break through. She declared ''he who reaches Marid has indeed attained power.''" restored on Marid Castle', '76a611f06d9bd17e6603bc2befa5a85a8cb95b95a8096e4b39311ace61261dd2|3dd98287bb887aa702ed4a3b78c2f5bb945d17140667d02d2d30b82259033afa|3dd98287bb887aa702ed4a3b78c2f5bb945d17140667d02d2d30b82259033afa', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-023", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On a hill over Dumat al-Jandal rises Marid Castle, stone below and mudbrick above. Queen Zenobia of Palmyra is reported to have besieged the city without being able to capture the fortress."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-023 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Marid Castle stands on a hill [S2]; it overlooks Dumat al-Jandal [S1, S2]; its lower part is stone [S6]; its upper part is mudbrick [S6]; Queen Zenobia of Palmyra is reported to have besieged the city [S4]; she is reported to have been unable to capture the fortress [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-069 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Marid Castle is a fortress (castle) at Dumat al-Jandal, Saudi Arabia [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marid_Castle]; It rises on a hill over Dumat al-Jandal [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marid_Castle]; It is built of stone below [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marid_Castle]; It is built of mudbrick above [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marid_Castle]; Queen Zenobia of Palmyra is reported to have besieged the city (Dumat al-Jandal) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marid_Castle]; She was unable to capture the fortress [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marid_Castle]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('be35e129-235b-498b-969d-580eac095ae0'::uuid, 'card_description', 'On a hillside above Tiverton, the Iron Age earthwork of Cranmore Castle overlooks the confluence of the Lowman and the Exe, its enclosure sloping from 120 metres to 170 metres.', NULL, 'teaser-card-s005-rollback:be35e129-235b-498b-969d-580eac095ae0:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s005: card_description None restored on Cranmore Castle', 'e01c4d36966c0cdf6e9ea0430ef2671c63c3e9cff097290f448667a102c21620|df8d859434c8562d835cc06e0214cd02064cabac983959a8b8aaf8b4fc7df4f6|df8d859434c8562d835cc06e0214cd02064cabac983959a8b8aaf8b4fc7df4f6', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-023", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On a hillside above Tiverton, the Iron Age earthwork of Cranmore Castle overlooks the confluence of the Lowman and the Exe, its enclosure sloping from 120 metres to 170 metres."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-023 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Cranmore Castle is on a hillside above Tiverton [S1]; it is an Iron Age earthwork [S1]; it overlooks the confluence of the Lowman and the Exe [S4]; its enclosure slopes from 120 metres to 170 metres [S2]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-069 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Cranmore Castle is an Iron Age earthwork, near Tiverton in England [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cranmore_Castle]; It lies on a hillside above Tiverton [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cranmore_Castle]; It overlooks the confluence of the Lowman and the Exe [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cranmore_Castle]; Its enclosure slopes, from 120 metres at its lowest [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cranmore_Castle]; Its enclosure rises to 170 metres at its highest [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cranmore_Castle]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('becd7970-8a7a-4a21-9832-889d66c4e5fc'::uuid, 'card_description', 'At 3,180 meters, east of the Cordillera Blanca, Sitio Arqueológico Chavín de Huántar was a gathering place where the people of the region came to worship, among temples and plazas.', 'The ceremonial heart of the Chavín culture (1500–300 BC), built as a truncated pyramid with a maze of interior stone passages and hidden galleries.', 'teaser-card-s005-rollback:becd7970-8a7a-4a21-9832-889d66c4e5fc:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s005: card_description ''The ceremonial heart of the Chavín culture (1500–300 BC), built as a truncated pyramid with a maze of interior stone passages and hidden galleries.'' restored on Sitio Arqueológico Chavín de Huántar', '2806f94b8b2df064bc143b4155ec4767b0a4867d78926ea9095020a0d33b63b5|a469be843f770f318d6f07e8136aec1bc20229f2550b52d8cafc89bf5a4cca25|a469be843f770f318d6f07e8136aec1bc20229f2550b52d8cafc89bf5a4cca25', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-023", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "At 3,180 meters, east of the Cordillera Blanca, Sitio Arqueológico Chavín de Huántar was a gathering place where the people of the region came to worship, among temples and plazas."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-023 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site is at an elevation of 3,180 meters [S2]; it is east of the Cordillera Blanca [S2]; it was a gathering place where the people of the region came to worship [S4]; it has temples [S6]; it has plazas [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-069 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Chavín de Huántar (Peru) was a gathering place where the people of the region came to worship [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chav%C3%ADn_de_Hu%C3%A1ntar]; It is an archaeological site in Peru [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chav%C3%ADn_de_Hu%C3%A1ntar]; It lies at 3,180 meters [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chav%C3%ADn_de_Hu%C3%A1ntar]; It lies east of the Cordillera Blanca [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chav%C3%ADn_de_Hu%C3%A1ntar]; The site has temples [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chav%C3%ADn_de_Hu%C3%A1ntar]; The site has plazas [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chav%C3%ADn_de_Hu%C3%A1ntar]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('bf6ada10-e9c6-49f0-b911-52e277f4368b'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Its name comes from a Maya meaning old walls: at Santa Rosa Xtampak in Campeche, the large Palace, the most unusual structure still standing there, holds 44 rooms in all.', 'A major Maya Chenes-region town that peaked in the Late Classic era. Its largest structure, "Hill Stars," has at least ten interconnected buildings.', 'teaser-card-s005-rollback:bf6ada10-e9c6-49f0-b911-52e277f4368b:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s005: card_description ''A major Maya Chenes-region town that peaked in the Late Classic era. Its largest structure, "Hill Stars," has at least ten interconnected buildings.'' restored on Santa Rosa Xtampak', '569b8a05354bff632442cb7cf80f86a7baa3264ac79f8a7c5ce8d4881e2fbaf7|1bf89e0801a18d05f5f2c4f393110a44e8ce7d3117a590f645169fc8f35f5a02|1bf89e0801a18d05f5f2c4f393110a44e8ce7d3117a590f645169fc8f35f5a02', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-023", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Its name comes from a Maya meaning old walls: at Santa Rosa Xtampak in Campeche, the large Palace, the most unusual structure still standing there, holds 44 rooms in all."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-023 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the name Xtampak comes from a Maya meaning old walls [S3]; Santa Rosa Xtampak is in Campeche [S1]; it has a large building called the Palace [S4]; the Palace is the most unusual structure still standing there [S4]; the Palace has 44 rooms in all [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-069 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Santa Rosa Xtampak is a Maya archaeological site in Campeche, Mexico [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xtampak]; Its name comes from a Maya word meaning old walls [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xtampak]; The site has a large Palace [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xtampak]; The Palace is the most unusual structure still standing at the site [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xtampak]; The Palace holds 44 rooms in all [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xtampak]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('bfa06d13-88b7-4e88-913b-1f06f7c5305b'::uuid, 'card_description', 'On the Agora of Athens, the Stoa Poikile, the Painted Portico, held paintings on tablets and bronze shields captured from the Spartans, and was linked with philosophy, especially Stoicism.', 'Doric stoa erected around 460 BC on the Agora''s north side, its name lives on in Stoicism—Zeno taught there. Its walls displayed trophy armour and the famous paintings of the Battle of Marathon.', 'teaser-card-s005-rollback:bfa06d13-88b7-4e88-913b-1f06f7c5305b:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s005: card_description "Doric stoa erected around 460 BC on the Agora''s north side, its name lives on in Stoicism—Zeno taught there. Its walls displayed trophy armour and the famous paintings of the Battle of Marathon." restored on Stoa Poikile', '691a3ac3892ce7e8f26075f2dfeb1bd67dcc8876b40db46fb22edecb68af2d90|5dfb39049707acd088ac4209f71ee2ce2560230e735d02def1ee21cb2ae125c9|5dfb39049707acd088ac4209f71ee2ce2560230e735d02def1ee21cb2ae125c9', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-023", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On the Agora of Athens, the Stoa Poikile, the Painted Portico, held paintings on tablets and bronze shields captured from the Spartans, and was linked with philosophy, especially Stoicism."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-023 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the Stoa Poikile is on the Agora of Athens [S1]; it is also called the Painted Portico [S1]; it held paintings on tablets [S5]; it held bronze shields captured from the Spartans [S6]; it was linked with philosophy, especially Stoicism [S2]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-069 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Stoa Poikile was a stoa (portico) on the Agora of Athens [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stoa_Poikile]; It is called the Painted Portico [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stoa_Poikile]; It held paintings on tablets [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stoa_Poikile]; It held bronze shields captured from the Spartans [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stoa_Poikile]; It was linked with philosophy, especially Stoicism [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stoa_Poikile]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('c0bca5b2-b29b-4810-bebe-9b7615778a1f'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Kings Weston Hill, a ridge about 1 mile long in the north of Bristol, rises between Kings Weston House and Blaise Castle. At its eastern end lies the site of an Iron Age hill fort.', 'Occupying the east end of a 1.6 km ridge above Bristol, this fort is now public open space managed as part of the Blaise Castle Estate. Ancient ramparts survive beneath the parkland surface.', 'teaser-card-s005-rollback:c0bca5b2-b29b-4810-bebe-9b7615778a1f:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s005: card_description ''Occupying the east end of a 1.6 km ridge above Bristol, this fort is now public open space managed as part of the Blaise Castle Estate. Ancient ramparts survive beneath the parkland surface.'' restored on Kings Weston Hill', '34004f0625a3553154e689f185e8ba6cb3b4c630f408f616fcebe450cad00e3b|d738c04dbe2654c329867121bff8a3d7ce1eba711b5d59884088d932753b685d|d738c04dbe2654c329867121bff8a3d7ce1eba711b5d59884088d932753b685d', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-024", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Kings Weston Hill, a ridge about 1 mile long in the north of Bristol, rises between Kings Weston House and Blaise Castle. At its eastern end lies the site of an Iron Age hill fort."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-024 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Kings Weston Hill is a ridge [S2]; the ridge is about 1 mile long [S2]; it lies in the north of Bristol [S1]; it rises between Kings Weston House and Blaise Castle [S4]; at its eastern end lies the site of a hill fort [S3]; the hill fort is Iron Age [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-070 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Kings Weston Hill is a hill in Bristol, England [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kings_Weston_Hill]; The hill lies in the north of Bristol [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kings_Weston_Hill]; The hill is a ridge [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kings_Weston_Hill]; The ridge is about 1 mile long [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kings_Weston_Hill]; Kings Weston House lies on one side of the hill (it rises beside Kings Weston House) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kings_Weston_Hill]; Blaise Castle lies on the other side of the hill (it rises between Kings Weston House and Blaise Castle) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kings_Weston_Hill]; The hill holds the site of an Iron Age hill fort [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kings_Weston_Hill]; The hill fort site lies at the eastern end of the hill [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kings_Weston_Hill]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('c10a35c7-ca6a-49ad-b620-7467aaca6b61'::uuid, 'card_description', 'On a narrow ridge 365 metres up in Gwynedd, the Iron Age hillfort of Caer Euni was once enlarged with a large rock-cut ditch. Inside, the sites of about 25 round huts have been detected.', 'Narrow ridge fort at 365 metres in Gwynedd, elongated to 216 metres along its spine with steep slopes on every side. Natural terrain largely eliminated the need for elaborate artificial defences.', 'teaser-card-s005-rollback:c10a35c7-ca6a-49ad-b620-7467aaca6b61:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s005: card_description ''Narrow ridge fort at 365 metres in Gwynedd, elongated to 216 metres along its spine with steep slopes on every side. Natural terrain largely eliminated the need for elaborate artificial defences.'' restored on Caer Euni', 'e357871efaef20e6feb0ffd59c7a9006d66c9c1a218e49bed60aa3d49bf805db|14a05d5af3b603d5f9123a022e3889745cadb7a76afccbab5d65e0ca84f359e8|14a05d5af3b603d5f9123a022e3889745cadb7a76afccbab5d65e0ca84f359e8', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-024", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On a narrow ridge 365 metres up in Gwynedd, the Iron Age hillfort of Caer Euni was once enlarged with a large rock-cut ditch. Inside, the sites of about 25 round huts have been detected."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-024 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Caer Euni stands on a narrow ridge [S2]; it is 365 metres up [S2]; it is in Gwynedd [S1]; it is an Iron Age hillfort [S1]; the fort was once enlarged, with further defences including a large rock-cut ditch [S6]; inside the enclosure the sites of about 25 round huts have been detected [S7]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-070 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Caer Euni is an Iron Age hillfort in Gwynedd, Wales [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caer_Euni]; Caer Euni is a hillfort [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caer_Euni]; The hillfort dates to the Iron Age [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caer_Euni]; It lies in Gwynedd [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caer_Euni]; It stands on a narrow ridge [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caer_Euni]; The ridge site is 365 metres up [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caer_Euni]; The fort was once enlarged [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caer_Euni]; The enlargement included a large rock-cut ditch [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caer_Euni]; Inside, the sites of about 25 round huts have been detected [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caer_Euni]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('c13dae70-fbe5-41bf-9679-57fd17343e4a'::uuid, 'card_description', 'On a steep slope of Kittern Hill, the highest point on Gugh in the Isles of Scilly, lies Obadiah''s Barrow, a Neolithic entrance grave and the largest on Gugh, excavated in 1901.', 'A Neolithic entrance grave on a steep slope, where excavation revealed a crouching male skeleton and Bronze Age cremation urns. It sits on the tiny island of Gugh in the Isles of Scilly.', 'teaser-card-s005-rollback:c13dae70-fbe5-41bf-9679-57fd17343e4a:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s005: card_description ''A Neolithic entrance grave on a steep slope, where excavation revealed a crouching male skeleton and Bronze Age cremation urns. It sits on the tiny island of Gugh in the Isles of Scilly.'' restored on Obadiah''s Barrow', '7b104a0e73fad142e5a5c8ce2c6c88df78bbd1afd4999b49eafe944d46699962|ba51d64cc3903fcb6348c3d760304ee672b9c58c700654bb2dd32b9e5855f140|ba51d64cc3903fcb6348c3d760304ee672b9c58c700654bb2dd32b9e5855f140', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-024", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On a steep slope of Kittern Hill, the highest point on Gugh in the Isles of Scilly, lies Obadiah''s Barrow, a Neolithic entrance grave and the largest on Gugh, excavated in 1901."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-024 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Obadiah''s Barrow lies on a steep slope of Kittern Hill [S2]; Kittern Hill is the highest point on Gugh [S2]; Gugh is in the Isles of Scilly [S1]; Obadiah''s Barrow is an entrance grave [S1]; it is Neolithic [S1]; it is the largest entrance grave on Gugh [S5]; it was excavated in 1901 [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-070 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Obadiah''s Barrow is an entrance grave on Gugh in the Isles of Scilly [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Obadiah%27s_Barrow]; It is a Neolithic entrance grave [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Obadiah%27s_Barrow]; Gugh lies in the Isles of Scilly [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Obadiah%27s_Barrow]; It lies on Kittern Hill [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Obadiah%27s_Barrow]; It lies on a steep slope of the hill [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Obadiah%27s_Barrow]; Kittern Hill is the highest point on Gugh [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Obadiah%27s_Barrow]; It is the largest entrance grave on Gugh [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Obadiah%27s_Barrow]; It was excavated in 1901 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Obadiah%27s_Barrow]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('c1f010f5-9a19-4070-8a1d-a3ddb408eac8'::uuid, 'card_description', 'On Malta''s southern coast, Mnajdra''s three conjoined temples are built of hard coralline limestone. The lowest is astronomically aligned, probably used to watch the sky or keep a calendar.', 'Mnajdra is made of coralline limestone, which is much harder than the soft globigerina limestone of Ħaġar Qim.', 'teaser-card-s005-rollback:c1f010f5-9a19-4070-8a1d-a3ddb408eac8:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s005: card_description ''Mnajdra is made of coralline limestone, which is much harder than the soft globigerina limestone of Ħaġar Qim.'' restored on Mnajdra', 'a9c8637023eb7b28642e6346ec6deebd1dad2ff3c5567eb863f4245138fa692d|69b2bd5f31240e6d1f61b7131f6023473e88ae1dbeb9ad9a3e1121551d12f1fd|69b2bd5f31240e6d1f61b7131f6023473e88ae1dbeb9ad9a3e1121551d12f1fd', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-024", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On Malta''s southern coast, Mnajdra''s three conjoined temples are built of hard coralline limestone. The lowest is astronomically aligned, probably used to watch the sky or keep a calendar."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-024 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Mnajdra is on Malta''s southern coast [S1]; Mnajdra consists of three temples [S5]; the temples are conjoined [S5]; they are built of coralline limestone [S4]; the coralline limestone is hard [S4]; the lowest temple is astronomically aligned [S7]; the lowest temple was probably used for astronomical observation or as a calendar [S7]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-070 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Mnajdra is a megalithic temple complex on the southern coast of Malta [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mnajdra]; It lies on Malta''s southern coast [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mnajdra]; It consists of three temples [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mnajdra]; The three temples are conjoined [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mnajdra]; The temples are built of coralline limestone [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mnajdra]; The coralline limestone is hard [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mnajdra]; The lowest temple is astronomically aligned [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mnajdra]; The lowest temple was probably used to watch the sky (astronomical observation) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mnajdra]; The lowest temple was probably used to keep a calendar [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mnajdra]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('c254f03d-ccce-4588-8094-5a11fbe67b6f'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Archaeology has probed the karst Tsutskhvati Cave Natural Monument in Imereti since the 1970s, when the State Museum of History of Georgia and the Institute of Paleobiology worked there.', 'Neanderthal cave at 320 m above the Chishura River, inhabited from ~48,000 BC. This karst cave yielded artefacts confirming human presence in the Imereti highlands long before farming began.', 'teaser-card-s005-rollback:c254f03d-ccce-4588-8094-5a11fbe67b6f:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s005: card_description ''Neanderthal cave at 320 m above the Chishura River, inhabited from ~48,000 BC. This karst cave yielded artefacts confirming human presence in the Imereti highlands long before farming began.'' restored on Tsutskhvati Cave Natural Monument', '40c5349b73f6ba77e4d8d8db1521a5e01f8965aa0652c0f17951766a19aca487|d86983364377b0ad231eb7680ecdd4db02c7c9035051496716abdebdab6e2652|d86983364377b0ad231eb7680ecdd4db02c7c9035051496716abdebdab6e2652', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-024", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Archaeology has probed the karst Tsutskhvati Cave Natural Monument in Imereti since the 1970s, when the State Museum of History of Georgia and the Institute of Paleobiology worked there."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-024 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Tsutskhvati Cave Natural Monument is a karst cave [S1]; it is in Imereti [S1]; archaeological study has been carried out there since the 1970s [S2]; in the 1970s the State Museum of History of Georgia and the Institute of Paleobiology worked at the site [S2]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-070 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Tsutskhvati Cave Natural Monument is a karst cave (an archaeological cave site) in Imereti, Georgia [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tsutskhvati_Cave_Natural_Monument]; It is a karst cave [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tsutskhvati_Cave_Natural_Monument]; It lies in Imereti [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tsutskhvati_Cave_Natural_Monument]; Archaeology has probed the cave (it is a site of archaeological work) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tsutskhvati_Cave_Natural_Monument]; Archaeological work there has gone on since the 1970s [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tsutskhvati_Cave_Natural_Monument]; The State Museum of History of Georgia worked there in the 1970s [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tsutskhvati_Cave_Natural_Monument]; The Institute of Paleobiology worked there in the 1970s [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tsutskhvati_Cave_Natural_Monument]; The two institutions worked there together [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tsutskhvati_Cave_Natural_Monument]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('c292f83a-933b-40e3-a658-ad7aa6c19d33'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Formed by an underground river, Les Combarelles was home to Cro-Magnon people approximately 13,000 to 11,000 years ago. Its galleries hold more than 600 engravings of animals and symbols.', 'Over 600 engravings of horses, mammoths, and reindeer — made roughly 13,000 years ago — fill two galleries here. Their density helped prove Cro-Magnons produced sophisticated art.', 'teaser-card-s005-rollback:c292f83a-933b-40e3-a658-ad7aa6c19d33:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s005: card_description ''Over 600 engravings of horses, mammoths, and reindeer — made roughly 13,000 years ago — fill two galleries here. Their density helped prove Cro-Magnons produced sophisticated art.'' restored on Les Combarelles', 'cce93f903ed90ccee49c8fb60a9faead658f77c97e112326780c606298b5a532|e6f957caacf6c2175c0d7f300bce09f21412f9c81a08794d71ca64f60ff0e0c6|e6f957caacf6c2175c0d7f300bce09f21412f9c81a08794d71ca64f60ff0e0c6', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-024", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Formed by an underground river, Les Combarelles was home to Cro-Magnon people approximately 13,000 to 11,000 years ago. Its galleries hold more than 600 engravings of animals and symbols."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-024 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Les Combarelles was formed by an underground river [S4]; it was inhabited by Cro-Magnon people [S1, S7]; the habitation was approximately 13,000 to 11,000 years ago [S1]; it has galleries holding engravings [S2]; there are more than 600 engravings [S2]; the engravings show animals and symbols [S2]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-071 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Les Combarelles is a cave (with galleries) in France [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Les_Combarelles]; The cave was formed by an underground river [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Les_Combarelles]; Les Combarelles was home to (inhabited by) Cro-Magnon people [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Les_Combarelles]; The occupation dates to approximately 13,000 to 11,000 years ago [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Les_Combarelles]; The site has (two) galleries holding the engravings [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Les_Combarelles]; The galleries hold more than 600 engravings [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Les_Combarelles]; The engravings depict animals and symbols [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Les_Combarelles]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('c3fda873-10be-4040-878f-350d6da7d70d'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Since the 1st century AD a single Roman arch of 17.20 m has spanned the river Ouvèze. The Roman Bridge is still in use, surviving severe floods that swept away some more recent bridges.', 'The bridge was built by the Romans in the 1st century AD, with a single arch spanning 17.20 m.', 'teaser-card-s005-rollback:c3fda873-10be-4040-878f-350d6da7d70d:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s005: card_description ''The bridge was built by the Romans in the 1st century AD, with a single arch spanning 17.20 m.'' restored on Roman Bridge, Vaison-la-Romaine', 'cdab39912e5d22b6d6d933c6e98c39e630de334c586daea4b7da9abb2406fc01|5de7bcde074fae197db7c7f615a5d104f664a7d2c2b4f928d791a7de61d8e49a|5de7bcde074fae197db7c7f615a5d104f664a7d2c2b4f928d791a7de61d8e49a', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-024", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Since the 1st century AD a single Roman arch of 17.20 m has spanned the river Ouvèze. The Roman Bridge is still in use, surviving severe floods that swept away some more recent bridges."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-024 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the bridge dates from the 1st century AD [S2]; it is Roman [S1, S2]; it has a single arch [S2]; the arch spans 17.20 m [S2]; it spans the river Ouveze [S1]; the Roman Bridge is still in use [S3]; it survived severe floods that swept away some more recent bridges [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-071 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Roman Bridge is a Roman bridge over the river Ouvèze at Vaison-la-Romaine, France [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Bridge_(Vaison-la-Romaine)]; The bridge dates from the 1st century AD [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Bridge_(Vaison-la-Romaine)]; The bridge has a single arch [SUPPORTED: https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pont_romain_de_Vaison-la-Romaine]; The arch spans 17.20 m [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Bridge_(Vaison-la-Romaine)]; The bridge is still in use [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Bridge_(Vaison-la-Romaine)]; The bridge has survived severe floods [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Bridge_(Vaison-la-Romaine)]; Those floods swept away some more recent bridges [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Bridge_(Vaison-la-Romaine)]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('c4dcfccd-1092-41dd-a29a-d3a0355bc325'::uuid, 'card_description', 'On a large mound platform at the Chiripa Archaeological site, a sunken plaza with a carved stone at its centre is surrounded by thatched houses with double walls of cobble and adobe.', 'A lakeside temple mound from 1400–850 BC with a sunken plaza and carved central stone. Rituals here predated the later Tiwanaku civilization by centuries.', 'teaser-card-s005-rollback:c4dcfccd-1092-41dd-a29a-d3a0355bc325:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s005: card_description ''A lakeside temple mound from 1400–850 BC with a sunken plaza and carved central stone. Rituals here predated the later Tiwanaku civilization by centuries.'' restored on Chiripa Archaeological site', 'f633e1a19f3f687ce3bc49a01600bee978c4cf5016ec02ccf9563436441e7875|927e466a59107e8b8fd2a9f0b67703f4fef0dda418ae17042749152c787d6264|927e466a59107e8b8fd2a9f0b67703f4fef0dda418ae17042749152c787d6264', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-024", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On a large mound platform at the Chiripa Archaeological site, a sunken plaza with a carved stone at its centre is surrounded by thatched houses with double walls of cobble and adobe."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-024 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the Chiripa Archaeological site has a large mound platform [S1]; on the platform is a sunken plaza [S2]; a carved stone stands at the centre of the plaza [S2]; the plaza is surrounded by houses [S4]; the houses have thatched roofs [S4]; the houses have double walls of cobble and adobe [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-071 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Chiripa is an archaeological settlement site in Bolivia [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chiripa_culture]; The site has a large mound platform [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chiripa_culture]; A sunken plaza lies on the mound platform [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chiripa_culture]; A carved stone stands at the centre of the sunken plaza [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chiripa_culture]; The sunken plaza is surrounded by houses [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chiripa_culture]; The houses were thatched [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chiripa_culture]; The houses had double walls of cobble and adobe [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chiripa_culture]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('c5b89e54-5127-4500-b6b4-e63f35efdc8d'::uuid, 'card_description', 'High on the left bank of the river Kuntichaka, a tributary of the Utcubamba, about 1,900 metres up, lies Machu Pirqa, a site of the Chachapoyas culture in the Amazonas Region of Peru.', '900–1470 AD. A Chachapoya settlement on the left bank of the Kuntichaka River at 1,900 m — part of the cloud-forest civilization that flourished in the Amazonas highlands before the Inca.', 'teaser-card-s005-rollback:c5b89e54-5127-4500-b6b4-e63f35efdc8d:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s005: card_description ''900–1470 AD. A Chachapoya settlement on the left bank of the Kuntichaka River at 1,900 m — part of the cloud-forest civilization that flourished in the Amazonas highlands before the Inca.'' restored on Machu Pirqa', '624e935959dc3bd6abb6c3162f13fc9fec6e4a9be6278155a401cda24cfe0c34|90468862e6e64eca1145d2a0a2012a2ad03d381a3b4c01136e89152aedc6d7e2|90468862e6e64eca1145d2a0a2012a2ad03d381a3b4c01136e89152aedc6d7e2', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-024", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "High on the left bank of the river Kuntichaka, a tributary of the Utcubamba, about 1,900 metres up, lies Machu Pirqa, a site of the Chachapoyas culture in the Amazonas Region of Peru."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-024 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Machu Pirqa lies on the left bank of the river Kuntichaka [S3]; the Kuntichaka is a tributary of the Utcubamba [S3]; it lies about 1,900 metres up (high) [S3]; it is a site of the Chachapoyas culture [S1]; it is in the Amazonas Region [S2]; it is in Peru [S1]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-071 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Machu Pirqa is an archaeological site of the Chachapoyas culture in Peru [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Machu_Pirqa]; Machu Pirqa lies in the Amazonas Region of Peru [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Machu_Pirqa]; Machu Pirqa lies on the left bank of the river [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Machu_Pirqa]; The river beside it is the Kuntichaka [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Machu_Pirqa]; The Kuntichaka is a tributary of the Utcubamba [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Machu_Pirqa]; The site lies high up [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Machu_Pirqa]; The site lies at about 1,900 metres elevation [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Machu_Pirqa]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('c5d05e95-f6a3-454f-85ba-6ebb4736b7bb'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Raised in Aosta in 25 BC to mark Rome''s victory over the Salassi, the Arch of Augustus later held the home of a local noble family and, in 1318, a small fort built inside it for crossbowmen.', 'During the 12th century, the arch contained the home of a local noble family and in 1318 a small fortification was built inside it, designed for a corps of crossbowmen.', 'teaser-card-s005-rollback:c5d05e95-f6a3-454f-85ba-6ebb4736b7bb:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s005: card_description ''During the 12th century, the arch contained the home of a local noble family and in 1318 a small fortification was built inside it, designed for a corps of crossbowmen.'' restored on Arch of Augustus', '15697cf98661479e1236a4b9b56f4eff55c5a32c3017bf01369ebccbd468ec5e|9b541518a429e2a99f452679504c81eac110afb4a8d5be6bc60cfcf69f5dfcb1|9b541518a429e2a99f452679504c81eac110afb4a8d5be6bc60cfcf69f5dfcb1', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-024", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Raised in Aosta in 25 BC to mark Rome''s victory over the Salassi, the Arch of Augustus later held the home of a local noble family and, in 1318, a small fort built inside it for crossbowmen."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-024 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the Arch of Augustus is in Aosta [S1]; it was raised in 25 BC [S2]; it marked Rome''s victory over the Salassi [S2]; it later held the home of a local noble family [S7]; in 1318 a small fortification was built inside it [S7]; the fortification was for crossbowmen [S7]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-071 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Arch of Augustus is a Roman (triumphal) arch in Aosta, Italy [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arch_of_Augustus_(Aosta)]; The arch was raised in 25 BC [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arch_of_Augustus_(Aosta)]; It was raised to mark Rome''s victory over the Salassi [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arch_of_Augustus_(Aosta)]; The arch later held the home of a local noble family [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arch_of_Augustus_(Aosta)]; In 1318 a small fort was built inside the arch [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arch_of_Augustus_(Aosta)]; The small fort was built for crossbowmen [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arch_of_Augustus_(Aosta)]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('c621bee7-f67e-4969-8bb0-d9b801d14e0f'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Pikimachay, a cave in the Ayacucho Valley, yielded some of the oldest plant remains in Peru, including an 11,000-year-old bottle gourd. Later layers held fishtail points, manos and metates.', 'Radiocarbon dates suggest presence here 22,200 to 14,700 years ago -- among the earliest in South America, though disputed. Part of the Ayacucho cave complex alongside Jaya Machay, the ''pepper cave''.', 'teaser-card-s005-rollback:c621bee7-f67e-4969-8bb0-d9b801d14e0f:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s005: card_description "Radiocarbon dates suggest presence here 22,200 to 14,700 years ago -- among the earliest in South America, though disputed. Part of the Ayacucho cave complex alongside Jaya Machay, the ''pepper cave''." restored on Pikimachay', 'ee9a2fad24a777ff618e0d60eb98b9f7f793154aca1d88b6baf028dcfdab6f0d|16c8f3af551b4be04a0ec555d745b5ba824d86c1d79fbe50153bb8ff2d2bbe15|16c8f3af551b4be04a0ec555d745b5ba824d86c1d79fbe50153bb8ff2d2bbe15', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-024", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Pikimachay, a cave in the Ayacucho Valley, yielded some of the oldest plant remains in Peru, including an 11,000-year-old bottle gourd. Later layers held fishtail points, manos and metates."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-024 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Pikimachay is a cave [S3]; it is in the Ayacucho Valley [S1]; it yielded some of the oldest plant remains in Peru [S4]; the remains include a bottle gourd [S4]; the bottle gourd is 11,000 years old [S4]; later layers held fishtail points [S5]; later layers held manos and metates [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-072 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Pikimachay is a cave (an archaeological cave site) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pikimachay]; Pikimachay lies in the Ayacucho Valley of Peru [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pikimachay]; Pikimachay yielded some of the oldest plant remains in Peru [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pikimachay]; Among the plant remains was an 11,000-year-old bottle gourd [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pikimachay]; Later layers held fishtail points [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pikimachay]; Later layers held manos [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pikimachay]; Later layers held metates [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pikimachay]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('c7328327-2d1b-4ffa-ae63-dca2ee6f688a'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Near Helsingborg, three large support stones of Gantoftadösen hold up a roof stone a meter thick, partially split in the middle and bearing nine cup marks. Funnelbeaker people raised it.', 'A Neolithic dolmen built between 3500 and 2800 BC by the Funnelbeaker culture. Three support stones hold up a 1-metre-thick capstone that has cracked down the middle.', 'teaser-card-s005-rollback:c7328327-2d1b-4ffa-ae63-dca2ee6f688a:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s005: card_description ''A Neolithic dolmen built between 3500 and 2800 BC by the Funnelbeaker culture. Three support stones hold up a 1-metre-thick capstone that has cracked down the middle.'' restored on Gantoftadösen', 'b9fa41398cc72c2a483fbb6c1a639a5932ffbaf59995450888f5afde3e936b25|40012f760e9c6359e2755c748edb329ffd01fe8da9d6a9a3eea849fc48f212b8|40012f760e9c6359e2755c748edb329ffd01fe8da9d6a9a3eea849fc48f212b8', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-024", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Near Helsingborg, three large support stones of Gantoftadösen hold up a roof stone a meter thick, partially split in the middle and bearing nine cup marks. Funnelbeaker people raised it."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-024 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Gantoftadosen is near Helsingborg [S1]; three large support stones hold up a roof stone [S4]; the roof stone is a meter thick [S4]; the roof stone is partially split in the middle [S4]; the roof stone bears nine cup marks [S5]; it was raised by Funnelbeaker people [S2]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-072 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Gantoftadösen is a dolmen (a megalithic tomb of support stones carrying a roof stone) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gantoftad%C3%B6sen]; It lies near Helsingborg [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gantoftad%C3%B6sen]; It has three large support stones [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gantoftad%C3%B6sen]; The support stones hold up a roof stone [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gantoftad%C3%B6sen]; The roof stone is a meter thick [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gantoftad%C3%B6sen]; The roof stone is partially split in the middle [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gantoftad%C3%B6sen]; The roof stone bears nine cup marks [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gantoftad%C3%B6sen]; Funnelbeaker people raised it [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gantoftad%C3%B6sen]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('c7994816-1b61-4c2b-a82b-513380825948'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Just below the ground at Carn Euny in Cornwall runs a fogou, an underground passage more than 65 feet long roofed with massive stone slabs. The remains of stone huts are still visible.', 'An Iron Age village with a fogou (underground passage) whose function is still debated. Stone huts replaced timber ones around 200 BC and remained in use for centuries.', 'teaser-card-s005-rollback:c7994816-1b61-4c2b-a82b-513380825948:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s005: card_description ''An Iron Age village with a fogou (underground passage) whose function is still debated. Stone huts replaced timber ones around 200 BC and remained in use for centuries.'' restored on Carn Euny', 'b19002eeb3c5d1c79da62d771b78dbf4770b335f6a47e45bff6c88698439f788|7765e4ed2500a4dba3f71f2788a79e596698c57bffef75cd178281a349391fac|7765e4ed2500a4dba3f71f2788a79e596698c57bffef75cd178281a349391fac', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-024", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Just below the ground at Carn Euny in Cornwall runs a fogou, an underground passage more than 65 feet long roofed with massive stone slabs. The remains of stone huts are still visible."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-024 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Carn Euny is in Cornwall [S1]; a fogou runs there just below the ground [S4, S5]; a fogou is an underground passage [S4]; the fogou is more than 65 feet long [S4]; it is roofed with massive stone slabs [S5]; the remains of stone huts are still visible [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-072 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Carn Euny is an ancient settlement site (an Iron Age and later village) with a fogou [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carn_Euny]; Carn Euny is in Cornwall [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carn_Euny]; Carn Euny has a fogou [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carn_Euny]; A fogou is an underground passage [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carn_Euny]; The fogou runs just below the ground [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carn_Euny]; The fogou is more than 65 feet long [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carn_Euny]; The fogou is roofed with massive stone slabs [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carn_Euny]; The remains of stone huts are still visible [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carn_Euny]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('c8d3dff0-b501-4ffa-8ef7-4f0605d8d091'::uuid, 'card_description', 'On a hill 3.2 kilometres west of Canterbury, Bigbury Camp is the only confirmed Iron Age hill fort in east Kent, where digging has unearthed iron agricultural tools and kitchen utensils.', 'The hill fort appears to have been first occupied around 350 BC, although the ditch-and-rampart defences were not built until the 2nd century BC.', 'teaser-card-s005-rollback:c8d3dff0-b501-4ffa-8ef7-4f0605d8d091:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s005: card_description ''The hill fort appears to have been first occupied around 350 BC, although the ditch-and-rampart defences were not built until the 2nd century BC.'' restored on Bigbury Camp', 'a3d83a74e4f8d739b40749589e7de6b5058a942105a21030b3032c9ed76362a3|e9647c42fbced30d7a3e60c035cc4061aacb9fccde286664a2127fa44b12bfde|e9647c42fbced30d7a3e60c035cc4061aacb9fccde286664a2127fa44b12bfde', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-024", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On a hill 3.2 kilometres west of Canterbury, Bigbury Camp is the only confirmed Iron Age hill fort in east Kent, where digging has unearthed iron agricultural tools and kitchen utensils."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-024 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Bigbury Camp is on a hill [S3]; it is 3.2 kilometres west of Canterbury [S3]; it is the only confirmed Iron Age hill fort in east Kent [S2]; digging there has unearthed iron artefacts including agricultural tools and kitchen utensils [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-072 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Bigbury Camp is a hill fort [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bigbury_Camp]; It dates from the Iron Age [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bigbury_Camp]; It lies on a hill [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bigbury_Camp]; It is 3.2 kilometres west of Canterbury [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bigbury_Camp]; It is the only confirmed Iron Age hill fort in east Kent [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bigbury_Camp]; Digging has unearthed iron agricultural tools [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bigbury_Camp]; Digging has unearthed iron kitchen utensils [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bigbury_Camp]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('c8e0a91d-298d-40f1-aef8-f8503e44b05a'::uuid, 'card_description', NULL, '1st-century AD Roman road, 39 km across the Fens from Denver, Norfolk to Peterborough. Fen Causeway linked East Anglia to the north and west, joining Ermine Street at its western end.', 'teaser-card-s005-rollback:c8e0a91d-298d-40f1-aef8-f8503e44b05a:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s005: card_description ''1st-century AD Roman road, 39 km across the Fens from Denver, Norfolk to Peterborough. Fen Causeway linked East Anglia to the north and west, joining Ermine Street at its western end.'' restored on Fen Causeway', '||5f894d3b2c07759df8cf2321f2e110fa56337a61d7e3fafa707796b9d4ede79e', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: contradicted-after-verify", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The web check could not prove: It runs across the marshy fens; The web check could not prove: It passes Eldernell; The web check contradicts: The road was up to 60 feet wide (https://www.heritage.norfolk.gov.uk/record-details?MNF2796-Fen-Causeway-Roman-road: \"It was described as very hard set gravel, three feet thick by 60 feet wide, and much barrelled. However, this has since been interpreted by R. R. Clarke as a roddon.\"); The web check contradicts: The road runs 24 miles (from Denver to its junction with Ermine Street) (https://www.twithr.co.uk/norfolk/fen-causeway.htm: \"Distance: 31 miles (Water Newton to Denver)\")"}]'::jsonb),
    ('c9358837-e3ee-4daa-aedc-34e45afa1692'::uuid, 'card_description', 'On Charlton Down in Dorset, Combs Ditch was once at least 6.4 kilometres long. Excavation here seems to show an Iron Age boundary ditch enlarged into a defensive earthwork.', 'The limited excavation seems to show an Iron Age boundary ditch being enlarged into a defensive earthwork in the late Roman or post-Roman period.', 'teaser-card-s005-rollback:c9358837-e3ee-4daa-aedc-34e45afa1692:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s005: card_description ''The limited excavation seems to show an Iron Age boundary ditch being enlarged into a defensive earthwork in the late Roman or post-Roman period.'' restored on Combs Ditch', '072fecd35adc48b166c40ffb3c141424fff5778080c92c2a346db213b05289d5|c095c843e6d659562d9843e3b76780500e7f9073b2a837075be2e73eea54030e|c095c843e6d659562d9843e3b76780500e7f9073b2a837075be2e73eea54030e', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-025", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On Charlton Down in Dorset, Combs Ditch was once at least 6.4 kilometres long. Excavation here seems to show an Iron Age boundary ditch enlarged into a defensive earthwork."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-025 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Combs Ditch is on Charlton Down [S1]; Charlton Down is in Dorset [S1]; Combs Ditch was once at least 6.4 kilometres long [S4]; excavation seems to show (hedged) an Iron Age boundary ditch [S8]; that boundary ditch was enlarged into a defensive earthwork (hedged) [S8]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-073 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Combs Ditch is a linear earthwork (a bank-and-ditch boundary/defensive earthwork) on Charlton Down in Dorset, England [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Combs_Ditch]; Combs Ditch was once at least 6.4 kilometres long [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Combs_Ditch]; Excavation has been carried out at Combs Ditch and its results are tentative (''seems to show'') [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Combs_Ditch]; The earthwork began as an Iron Age boundary ditch [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Combs_Ditch]; The Iron Age boundary ditch was later enlarged into a defensive earthwork [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Combs_Ditch]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('c947e9b6-41c7-4c94-81c3-d79fc9e601b2'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Just inland from Peru''s south coast, many adobe walls of the Inca complex Tambo Colorado still hold enough colored paint to reconstruct how they were once painted.', 'The site was most likely built at the end of the 15th century during the reign of the Inca king Pachacuti Inca Yupanqui also known as Pachacutec.', 'teaser-card-s005-rollback:c947e9b6-41c7-4c94-81c3-d79fc9e601b2:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s005: card_description ''The site was most likely built at the end of the 15th century during the reign of the Inca king Pachacuti Inca Yupanqui also known as Pachacutec.'' restored on Tambo Colorado', '60dbd2a54022758812d9998636e69dfee381f88c8ce3e35f4531c2da36548a52|536998d2745110199d0a8f32db6f2908cc88f4f5f55e4333e13300e20fa45f4e|536998d2745110199d0a8f32db6f2908cc88f4f5f55e4333e13300e20fa45f4e', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-025", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Just inland from Peru''s south coast, many adobe walls of the Inca complex Tambo Colorado still hold enough colored paint to reconstruct how they were once painted."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-025 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Tambo Colorado lies just inland from Peru''s south coast [S2]; Tambo Colorado is an Inca complex [S1]; its walls are adobe [S1]; many walls still hold enough colored paint to reconstruct how they were once painted [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-073 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Tambo Colorado is an Inca complex in Peru [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tambo_Colorado]; Tambo Colorado lies just inland from Peru''s south coast [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tambo_Colorado]; The walls of Tambo Colorado are built of adobe [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tambo_Colorado]; Many walls at Tambo Colorado still retain colored paint [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tambo_Colorado]; The surviving paint is enough to reconstruct how the walls were originally painted [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tambo_Colorado]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('c96274da-bdec-445d-a79f-2a43c0688421'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Neanderthal hunters once sheltered in Escoural Cave to hunt aurochs, deer and horse. Later came Paleolithic rock art, burials in its interior and a settlement that became a fortified site.', 'A cave with Paleolithic rock art and human traces stretching back 50,000 years. Neanderthal hunter-gatherers were its first occupants, followed by modern humans.', 'teaser-card-s005-rollback:c96274da-bdec-445d-a79f-2a43c0688421:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s005: card_description ''A cave with Paleolithic rock art and human traces stretching back 50,000 years. Neanderthal hunter-gatherers were its first occupants, followed by modern humans.'' restored on Escoural Cave', '50883d4042bf4f244168ddb0a3eb7550e48d52c6d02868cd29d30856edab72b6|b32968d39ffac0973b2ec19024fc894a2801def381d9e0a1c5d20c35c03c49bb|b32968d39ffac0973b2ec19024fc894a2801def381d9e0a1c5d20c35c03c49bb', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-025", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Neanderthal hunters once sheltered in Escoural Cave to hunt aurochs, deer and horse. Later came Paleolithic rock art, burials in its interior and a settlement that became a fortified site."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-025 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Neanderthal hunters once sheltered in Escoural Cave [S3]; they sheltered there in order to hunt [S3]; they hunted aurochs, deer and horse [S4]; Paleolithic rock art came later than the first Neanderthal occupants [S1, S2, S3, S5]; the interior was used for burials [S1, S5]; a settlement at the cave later became a fortified site [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-073 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Escoural Cave in Portugal is a prehistoric cave site known for Paleolithic rock art and burials [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Escoural_Cave]; Neanderthal hunters were among the cave''s occupants (its first occupants) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Escoural_Cave]; The Neanderthals used the cave as a shelter in order to hunt [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Escoural_Cave]; They hunted aurochs, deer and horse [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Escoural_Cave]; Later the cave received Paleolithic rock art [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Escoural_Cave]; Burials were later made in the cave''s interior [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Escoural_Cave]; A settlement came later at the site [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Escoural_Cave]; That settlement developed into a fortified site [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Escoural_Cave]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('ca012875-d72b-4d00-99a7-0246f1769a32'::uuid, 'card_description', 'An island known as Snake Island or Pelican Island, Golem Grad keeps ancient ruins and churches, uninhabited since its small monastic community left in the mid-20th century.', 'A 20-hectare island in Lake Prespa covered in ancient walls, medieval churches, and dense snake colonies — earning the nickname "Snake Island." It opened to tourism in August 2008.', 'teaser-card-s005-rollback:ca012875-d72b-4d00-99a7-0246f1769a32:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s005: card_description ''A 20-hectare island in Lake Prespa covered in ancient walls, medieval churches, and dense snake colonies — earning the nickname "Snake Island." It opened to tourism in August 2008.'' restored on Golem Grad', '0b8e27dac420e770ebc62b2c046be79b50f4afe18aa1de5e9118a2c205bce334|3383a80064febba169aef946f38c929490b08510fab72a0aee2c16851dea76ff|3383a80064febba169aef946f38c929490b08510fab72a0aee2c16851dea76ff', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-025", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "An island known as Snake Island or Pelican Island, Golem Grad keeps ancient ruins and churches, uninhabited since its small monastic community left in the mid-20th century."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-025 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Golem Grad is an island [S1]; it is known as Snake Island or Pelican Island [S1]; it has ancient ruins and churches [S2]; it has been uninhabited since the mid-20th century [S3]; it became uninhabited when its small monastic community left [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-073 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Golem Grad is an island (in North Macedonia) with ancient remains [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Golem_Grad]; Golem Grad is also known as Snake Island [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Golem_Grad]; Golem Grad is also known as Pelican Island [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Golem_Grad]; The island has ancient ruins and churches [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Golem_Grad]; The island is uninhabited today [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Golem_Grad]; It has been uninhabited since the mid-20th century [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Golem_Grad]; It became uninhabited when its small monastic community left the island [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Golem_Grad]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('cae9d762-cdd4-4833-adc3-c426b7a79948'::uuid, 'card_description', 'On the bank of a dried-up riverbed lies Aynuna: a trading post dated from the Roman period to early Islamic times, and on the plateau cut by the wadi, a fortified settlement.', NULL, 'teaser-card-s005-rollback:cae9d762-cdd4-4833-adc3-c426b7a79948:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s005: card_description None restored on Aynuna', 'a6cbb3fbd60de619428ffd507ba15b3c2cac993c91ad663f7dafb659be0ce002|80aeff1c51dbdbebcdd6e6ba83e07a5b5f0f0fe550a7df4d274f4986593d9b00|80aeff1c51dbdbebcdd6e6ba83e07a5b5f0f0fe550a7df4d274f4986593d9b00', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-025", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On the bank of a dried-up riverbed lies Aynuna: a trading post dated from the Roman period to early Islamic times, and on the plateau cut by the wadi, a fortified settlement."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-025 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Aynuna lies on the bank of a dried-up riverbed [S2]; Aynuna has a trading post [S3]; the trading post is dated from the Roman period to early Islamic times [S3]; the plateau is cut by the wadi [S2, S4]; there is a fortified settlement on that plateau [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-073 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Aynuna (Saudi Arabia) is an archaeological site of an ancient trading post [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aynuna]; Aynuna lies on the bank of a dried-up riverbed [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aynuna]; The trading post is dated from the Roman period [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aynuna]; The trading post is dated up to early Islamic times [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aynuna]; The wadi (dried-up riverbed) cuts through a plateau [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aynuna]; On that plateau lie the remains of a fortified settlement [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aynuna]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('cb2f9081-0b6b-469c-ba0d-16d21149fa3a'::uuid, 'card_description', 'In 1754 William Borlase drew Boswens Menhir standing in a small low cairn. The stone still rises 2.4 metres in Penwith, Cornwall, but its cairn is now only just noticeable.', NULL, 'teaser-card-s005-rollback:cb2f9081-0b6b-469c-ba0d-16d21149fa3a:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s005: card_description None restored on Boswens Menhir', '0740571c7f496e7ad736fea1c71afb95ef27e51f5aa8783d7ba519bb8048f68c|bcec53d99ac3e523209c32acd9ba7a879d196ed73883c8bcbe60e141b6a717ce|bcec53d99ac3e523209c32acd9ba7a879d196ed73883c8bcbe60e141b6a717ce', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-025", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "In 1754 William Borlase drew Boswens Menhir standing in a small low cairn. The stone still rises 2.4 metres in Penwith, Cornwall, but its cairn is now only just noticeable."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-025 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "in 1754 William Borlase drew (illustrated) the stone [S7]; Borlase showed it standing in a small low cairn [S7]; the stone is 2.4 metres high [S5]; it stands in Penwith, Cornwall [S1, S3]; its cairn is now only just noticeable [S8]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-074 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Boswens Menhir is a standing stone (menhir) in Penwith, Cornwall [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boswens_Menhir]; The stone lies in Penwith [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boswens_Menhir]; In 1754 William Borlase drew (illustrated) the stone [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boswens_Menhir]; Borlase showed the stone standing in a small low cairn [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boswens_Menhir]; The stone still stands and is 2.4 metres high [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boswens_Menhir]; The cairn is now only just noticeable [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boswens_Menhir]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('cc277657-9ec6-4579-8c7e-678abf1c4a37'::uuid, 'card_description', 'In a little wood on Bornholm, the tall uninscribed stones of Gryet stand among the trees over graves of burnt bones, on high ground that was once surrounded by water or marshes.', 'One of the island''s largest collections of megaliths, with tall stones standing among trees on high ground. Once over 60 stones; many have fallen or been removed.', 'teaser-card-s005-rollback:cc277657-9ec6-4579-8c7e-678abf1c4a37:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s005: card_description "One of the island''s largest collections of megaliths, with tall stones standing among trees on high ground. Once over 60 stones; many have fallen or been removed." restored on Gryet', '5c1edee63ca85ef308a0e890e07ec63665b3a585af0924cc67f72872b1860898|b848eac227b195b17ed76e3290120407cc186866ad5a9496ba6c7fb78e85c8b6|b848eac227b195b17ed76e3290120407cc186866ad5a9496ba6c7fb78e85c8b6', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-rewrite-v-003", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "In a little wood on Bornholm, the tall uninscribed stones of Gryet stand among the trees over graves of burnt bones, on high ground that was once surrounded by water or marshes."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-v-003 (check-v), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the stones stand in a little wood [S1]; Gryet is on Bornholm [S1]; the stones are tall [S1]; the stones bear no inscription [S6]; the stones stand among the trees [S1]; the stones stand over graves holding burnt bones [S6]; the site is on high ground [S2]; the high ground was once surrounded by water or marshes [S2]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify2-007 (verify2), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Gryet is a site of standing stones (megaliths/bauta stones) marking graves, on Bornholm, Denmark [SUPPORTED: https://da.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gryet]; Gryet is on the island of Bornholm [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gryet]; The stones are in a little wood, standing among the trees [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gryet]; The stones are tall [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gryet]; The stones are uninscribed [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gryet]; The stones stand over graves of burnt bones [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gryet]; Gryet is on high ground [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gryet]; The high ground was once surrounded by water or marshes [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gryet]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('cc5d8fbd-741d-4da3-9ac4-c7bd797d4fd8'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Almost at the entrance to the Messina Strait, Tauriana has given up road axes, houses, drainage channels and dolia for foodstuffs: a town between the 4th and the 1st century BC.', 'An ancient Bruttian city on the border between Reggio and Locri. Its Tyrrhenian coast position made it a flashpoint between rival Greek colonies.', 'teaser-card-s005-rollback:cc5d8fbd-741d-4da3-9ac4-c7bd797d4fd8:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s005: card_description ''An ancient Bruttian city on the border between Reggio and Locri. Its Tyrrhenian coast position made it a flashpoint between rival Greek colonies.'' restored on Tauriana', '6d654d2b26000a464d0c32aed339792a838db18811104ef4d64afaa81b4ef0a4|208001a905b6926edc45d3cb07dfb23fa949a0c7769b76dbab98461a2902b2b5|208001a905b6926edc45d3cb07dfb23fa949a0c7769b76dbab98461a2902b2b5', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-025", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Almost at the entrance to the Messina Strait, Tauriana has given up road axes, houses, drainage channels and dolia for foodstuffs: a town between the 4th and the 1st century BC."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-025 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Tauriana''s location is almost at the entrance to the Messina Strait [S4]; research has exposed road axes, houses and drainage channels there [S6]; research has exposed dolia for foodstuffs there [S6]; the exposed town dates between the 4th (second half) and the 1st century BC [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-074 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Tauriana is an ancient town (city), today in the territory of Palmi, Calabria [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tauriana]; Tauriana lies almost at the entrance to the Messina Strait [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tauriana]; Excavations have been carried out at Tauriana (on the Taureana plateau) [SUPPORTED: https://sabap-rc.cultura.gov.it/?page_id=185]; The finds include road axes (assi stradali) [SUPPORTED: https://sabap-rc.cultura.gov.it/?page_id=185]; The finds include houses [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tauriana]; The finds include drainage channels [SUPPORTED: https://sabap-rc.cultura.gov.it/?page_id=185]; The finds include dolia for foodstuffs [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tauriana]; The excavated town dates between the 4th and the 1st century BC [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tauriana]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('cc8b3804-e27e-4dc3-b6bf-5ffeeaef0018'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Ratae Corieltauvorum, the ramparts of the Corieltauvians, is Leicester today. Excavations since 2016 have revealed luxurious, highly decorated houses at the heart of the Roman town.', 'A Roman town that grew from a native settlement in the 1st century BC. Today it lies beneath modern Leicester — where Richard III''s body was found.', 'teaser-card-s005-rollback:cc8b3804-e27e-4dc3-b6bf-5ffeeaef0018:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s005: card_description "A Roman town that grew from a native settlement in the 1st century BC. Today it lies beneath modern Leicester — where Richard III''s body was found." restored on Ratae Corieltauvorum', 'b30a697e99229ff51668b53e4b17ea2765de5346e224eb357aa4e22d207db25d|3b3350c5f1b098ff136094ebc6a2e34b5b0fdee15d5da4eaa7e864152d8a117f|3b3350c5f1b098ff136094ebc6a2e34b5b0fdee15d5da4eaa7e864152d8a117f', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-025", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Ratae Corieltauvorum, the ramparts of the Corieltauvians, is Leicester today. Excavations since 2016 have revealed luxurious, highly decorated houses at the heart of the Roman town."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-025 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Ratae means ramparts [S3]; Corieltauvorum means of the Corieltauvians [S4]; Ratae Corieltauvorum is Leicester today [S2]; excavations since 2016 have revealed luxurious, highly decorated houses [S6]; the houses are in central locations of the town [S6]; it was a Roman town [S1]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-074 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Ratae Corieltauvorum was a Roman town, which is Leicester today [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ratae_Corieltauvorum]; Ratae means ''ramparts'' [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ratae_Corieltauvorum]; Corieltauvorum means ''of the Corieltauvians'' [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ratae_Corieltauvorum]; Excavations began in 2016 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ratae_Corieltauvorum]; The excavations revealed luxurious houses [SUPPORTED: https://ulasnews.com/2017/12/01/roman-mosaic-excavation-in-leicester-nominated-for-archaeology-award/]; The houses were highly decorated [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ratae_Corieltauvorum]; The houses lie at the heart (in central locations) of the Roman town [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ratae_Corieltauvorum]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('ccaa2834-9ed2-4ae3-91e1-cf85e846d76d'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Habitancum took its name from a word inscribed on an altar. On a low mound above the River Rede, this Roman fort was destroyed and rebuilt again and again, finally falling in about 368.', 'A Roman fort along Dere Street, the military highway from York into Scotland. An altar found here records the name of a consular beneficiary stationed at the garrison.', 'teaser-card-s005-rollback:ccaa2834-9ed2-4ae3-91e1-cf85e846d76d:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s005: card_description ''A Roman fort along Dere Street, the military highway from York into Scotland. An altar found here records the name of a consular beneficiary stationed at the garrison.'' restored on Habitancum', '147c3091d103f2cff991a9a48a24e061312b9d7a9be4035e95b9790a785359ef|36ca6754b52dd975b0a3ea21fcfa367fd7076bbe737be7f12bb5ce029deaf74b|36ca6754b52dd975b0a3ea21fcfa367fd7076bbe737be7f12bb5ce029deaf74b', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-025", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Habitancum took its name from a word inscribed on an altar. On a low mound above the River Rede, this Roman fort was destroyed and rebuilt again and again, finally falling in about 368."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-025 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Habitancum took its name from a word inscribed on an altar [S2]; it lies on a low mound [S3]; it overlooks the River Rede [S3]; it is a Roman fort [S1]; it was destroyed and rebuilt repeatedly [S5, S6, S7]; it was finally destroyed in about 368 [S7]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-074 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Habitancum is a Roman fort (at Risingham, Northumberland) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Habitancum]; Habitancum took its name from a word inscribed on an altar [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Habitancum]; The fort stands on a low mound [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Habitancum]; The fort lies above the River Rede [SUPPORTED: https://www.roman-britain.co.uk/?places=risingham]; The fort was destroyed and rebuilt again and again [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Habitancum]; The fort finally fell (was finally destroyed) in about 368 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Habitancum]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('cd2e38bd-c70e-4813-8ccd-0a567ab6a7ca'::uuid, 'card_description', 'High on a Peruvian mountain, 3,580 metres up, Markahirka keeps cave paintings and stone tombs, and looks out over the Puchka valley to the towns of Huari and Huachis.', 'A mountaintop site at 3,580 metres with cave paintings and stone burial towers called chullpas. These above-ground tombs let Andean peoples keep their ancestors visible on the landscape.', 'teaser-card-s005-rollback:cd2e38bd-c70e-4813-8ccd-0a567ab6a7ca:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s005: card_description ''A mountaintop site at 3,580 metres with cave paintings and stone burial towers called chullpas. These above-ground tombs let Andean peoples keep their ancestors visible on the landscape.'' restored on Markahirka', '535d86d480f3794aaffa9d6f2f0335849e9ea395e2c909fab204aeab31189ad0|831f219ff2f6a81ebc209aced05bc1ece5d3fb19454c0eaa2d9319f0db228796|831f219ff2f6a81ebc209aced05bc1ece5d3fb19454c0eaa2d9319f0db228796', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-025", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "High on a Peruvian mountain, 3,580 metres up, Markahirka keeps cave paintings and stone tombs, and looks out over the Puchka valley to the towns of Huari and Huachis."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-025 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Markahirka is on a mountain in Peru [S1]; it is at a height of 3,580 metres [S3]; it has cave paintings [S1]; it has stone tombs [S1]; it has views into the Puchka valley [S4]; it has views to the towns of Huari and Huachis [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-075 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Markahirka is an archaeological site on a mountain in Peru [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Markahirka]; The site lies 3,580 metres up [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Markahirka]; Markahirka has cave paintings [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Markahirka]; Markahirka has stone tombs [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Markahirka]; It looks out over the Puchka valley [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Markahirka]; It looks out to the towns of Huari and Huachis [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Markahirka]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('cdd84f55-f2c9-41a9-8f15-72e6e3f4e69d'::uuid, 'card_description', 'The biblical Land of Tema has been identified with the oasis of Teima'', enriched by an ancient caravan road, where Nabunaid of Babylon captured the city and settled.', 'A 6th-century BC oasis mentioned in the Bible as the land of Ishmael''s descendants. Babylonian king Nabonidus lived here for ten years, abandoning his own capital.', 'teaser-card-s005-rollback:cdd84f55-f2c9-41a9-8f15-72e6e3f4e69d:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s005: card_description "A 6th-century BC oasis mentioned in the Bible as the land of Ishmael''s descendants. Babylonian king Nabonidus lived here for ten years, abandoning his own capital." restored on Land of Tema', 'ea3c61af5745e1884c17b8b82760d5c4d5f52ea70f905861889951054ea0f819|693d3ed0f46ada5b882824a36cb2cb771da0a3effcc35fdec323c6061217b605|693d3ed0f46ada5b882824a36cb2cb771da0a3effcc35fdec323c6061217b605', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-025", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The biblical Land of Tema has been identified with the oasis of Teima'', enriched by an ancient caravan road, where Nabunaid of Babylon captured the city and settled."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-025 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the Land of Tema is mentioned in the Bible [S1]; it has been identified with Teima'' [S2]; Teima'' is an oasis [S2]; an ancient caravan road brought wealth to the town [S3]; Nabunaid of Babylon captured the city [S6]; Nabunaid settled there [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-075 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Land of Tema is a biblical place identified with the oasis of Teima'' (Tayma) in Saudi Arabia [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Land_of_Tema]; Tema is a place of the Bible [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tayma]; Teima'' is an oasis [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tayma]; Teima'' lay on an ancient caravan road [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Land_of_Tema]; The caravan road enriched the town [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Land_of_Tema]; Nabunaid (Nabonidus) was king of Babylon [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tayma]; Nabunaid of Babylon captured the city [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nabonidus]; Nabunaid settled in the city [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nabonidus]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('cf2a3dbd-5118-4578-b4cd-24886ea997bd'::uuid, 'card_description', 'On the Danube at Tulln, the Roman fort of Comagena first housed up to 500 cavalry troops, and in its later days served as the base of a naval fleet as well as a cavalry regiment.', 'A Roman cavalry fort on the Danube guarding a critical ford. It housed 500 horsemen and controlled the valley lowlands of the frontier zone.', 'teaser-card-s005-rollback:cf2a3dbd-5118-4578-b4cd-24886ea997bd:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s005: card_description ''A Roman cavalry fort on the Danube guarding a critical ford. It housed 500 horsemen and controlled the valley lowlands of the frontier zone.'' restored on Comagena', '5629e9a2fe5e712acfae16a22251584b4f8f19bf4efe89ad09d217daf2b8a799|474c80fcbb71f1b659f3e8599013b4b710b95d3e51c45d0703ee7652bcee4d7e|474c80fcbb71f1b659f3e8599013b4b710b95d3e51c45d0703ee7652bcee4d7e', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-025", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On the Danube at Tulln, the Roman fort of Comagena first housed up to 500 cavalry troops, and in its later days served as the base of a naval fleet as well as a cavalry regiment."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-025 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Comagena is on the Danube [S1]; Comagena is at Tulln [S1]; Comagena was a Roman fort [S1]; it originally housed up to 500 cavalry troops [S2]; in its later days it was the base of a naval fleet [S4]; in its later days it was also the base of a cavalry regiment [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-075 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Comagena is a Roman fort at Tulln, Austria [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Comagena]; The fort stands on the Danube [SUPPORTED: https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kastell_Comagena]; The fort first housed cavalry troops [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Comagena]; The first garrison numbered up to 500 cavalry [SUPPORTED: https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kastell_Comagena]; In its later days the fort was the base of a naval fleet [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Comagena]; In its later days the fort was also the base of a cavalry regiment [SUPPORTED: https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kastell_Comagena]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('cf46928b-ed62-4b81-acbd-8822349ca58a'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Dug into the hill of Zlatno brdo, the Roman Tomb of Brestovik dates from circa 300 and was found by accident in 1895, its entrance on the east side, its walls covered with frescoes.', 'Roman tomb in Brestovik is an ancient tomb, dating from circa 300, which was discovered in 1895 in Brestovik, today a suburban village of Belgrade.', 'teaser-card-s005-rollback:cf46928b-ed62-4b81-acbd-8822349ca58a:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s005: card_description ''Roman tomb in Brestovik is an ancient tomb, dating from circa 300, which was discovered in 1895 in Brestovik, today a suburban village of Belgrade.'' restored on Roman Tomb, Brestovik', '8b2cccc1302035721d2f00c2f9c0dced947ab47e3a61e84afaf831124a743d6a|daae91076e6afbe923539ef6da9379893575afb466ceb0e1d82bcad49b9c0bca|daae91076e6afbe923539ef6da9379893575afb466ceb0e1d82bcad49b9c0bca', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-025", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Dug into the hill of Zlatno brdo, the Roman Tomb of Brestovik dates from circa 300 and was found by accident in 1895, its entrance on the east side, its walls covered with frescoes."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-025 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the tomb is dug into the hill of Zlatno brdo [S3]; it is a Roman tomb at Brestovik [S1, S2]; it dates from circa 300 [S1]; it was found by accident [S4]; it was found in 1895 [S1, S4]; its entrance is on the east side [S6]; its walls were covered with frescoes [S7]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-075 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The site is a Roman tomb at Brestovik, Serbia [SUPPO